
# >>> dinomem git-autosnapshot ignores >>>
# The snapshot store itself (its own object DB lives inside the work-tree) —
# MUST be ignored or every tick sees git internals as churn and self-commits.
.dinomem-snap.git/
.dinomem-snap.git
# ...AND the rebuild/orphan siblings. SIBLING-PREFIX TRAP (fixed 2026-10-08):
# the two patterns above do NOT match `.dinomem-snap.git.OLD-<ts>`, which is
# exactly where rebuild-store.sh parks the previous store -- inside the
# work-tree. MEASURED on workspace-sosmed: the rebuild dropped 144 commits to a
# clean 1-commit baseline with 0 lfs objects, and then `git add -A` committed
# the ENTIRE parked old store back in as ordinary blobs. Result: 197M -> 186M
# instead of ~3M, with 182M of dead LFS objects now welded into the FRESH
# history (worse than before -- they used to be prunable files, now they are
# reachable git objects). The 12 largest objects in the new pack were all
# `.dinomem-snap.git.OLD-*/lfs/objects/*`.
# Same class as the backup/backups near-miss. Match the siblings explicitly.
.dinomem-snap.git.*
.dinomem-snap.git.OLD-*/
.dinomem-snap.git.old-*/
# Runtime state that churns constantly and is never useful in git history.
# Agent runtime state (auto-generated)
agents/*/agent/models.json
agents/*/agent/plugins/*/catalog.json
agents/*/agent/*.sqlite
agents/*/agent/*.sqlite-shm
agents/*/agent/*.sqlite-wal
agents/*/agent/*.sqlite-journal
# Plain sqlite DBs anywhere (e.g. chroma.sqlite3 in vector stores). Previously
# only the -shm/-wal/-journal sidecars were ignored, so the 250MB main DB leaked.
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite-shm
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-journal
*.sqlite.tmp-*
*.sqlite.migrated
*.migrated
# Cron/gate last-run markers
scripts/.*.last_run
workspace-*/scripts/.*.last_run
# Cache/tee spill
memory/cache/
workspace-*/memory/cache/
logs/
# Vector DB / models / big runtime data (regenerable, huge).
# NOTE: neuron adds sibling stores kb/vector_db_sessions/ and kb/vector_db_docs/
# — the old `vector_db/` glob does NOT match those (different dir name, not a
# subdir). Use vector_db* to cover the whole family, present and future.
kb/vector_db*/
workspace-*/kb/vector_db*/
kb/memory_neuron/
workspace-*/kb/memory_neuron/
models/
workspace-*/models/
# Neuron code-structure graph: kb/code_graph/graph.db is a ~700MB derived,
# rebuildable index (AST/graph store) that rewrites on refresh — same churny/
# huge class as the vector DBs. The vector_db* glob does NOT match it (different
# dir), so it needs its own rule. Missing this was a real leak vector.
kb/code_graph/
workspace-*/kb/code_graph/
# Quarantined corrupt vector-DB dirs dinomem itself renames aside on a failed
# rebuild (kb/.vector_db_sessions.corrupt-<ts>/). Derived, huge, never useful.
kb/.vector_db*.corrupt-*/
workspace-*/kb/.vector_db*.corrupt-*/
# Rotated memory archive dinomem writes when it ages out daily notes.
.memory_archive/
workspace-*/.memory_archive/
# OpenClaw runtime dirs (dinomem runs inside OpenClaw, so these are always
# present + always churny/regenerable on a dinomem host): staged inbound media,
# per-run cache, and transient runtime state.
cache/
workspace-*/cache/
runtime/
workspace-*/runtime/
media/inbound/
workspace-*/media/inbound/
# git-autosnapshot's own log
logs/git-autosnapshot.log
# Generic dev-junk hygiene. WHY: the rules above only cover dinomem's OWN
# derived data (vector DBs, sqlite, models). A workspace can also accrue
# standard dev cruft — a Python venv, node_modules, __pycache__, .env secrets —
# which is huge/churny/regenerable and must never enter snapshot history.
# Without these, dropping a venv or running `npm install` in a snapshotted
# workspace would leak exactly the way the vector DBs did.
.venv/
venv/
env/
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/site-packages/
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.egg-info/
**/*.egg-info/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
# Secrets — never snapshot credential files.
.env
*.env
**/.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
# Convention-junk on a dinomem/OpenClaw host: local backup dirs and loose *.db
# dumps. NOTE (tradeoff): these are broader than dinomem's own artifacts — if
# YOU deliberately keep a `backups/` dir or a `data/*.db` you want versioned,
# allowlist it via `.dinomem-keep-large` or remove these lines from this store's
# info/exclude. Default-ignored here because on a standard box they're
# regenerable junk, and silently bloating .git is the worse failure.
backup/
backups/
workspace-*/backup/
workspace-*/backups/
data/*.db
workspace-*/data/*.db

# ── BINARIES / MEDIA: IGNORED, NOT LFS-TRACKED (2026-10-08) ────────────────
#
# These globs used to live in gitattributes.template as `filter=lfs` rules. That
# made a CHANGELOG behave like a BACKUP: auto-commit.sh's size guard
# (AUTOSNAP_MAX_MB) deliberately EXEMPTED LFS-tracked paths, so every binary
# bypassed the guard and git-lfs kept a byte-exact copy of it forever in
# .dinomem-snap.git/lfs/objects.
#
# MEASURED on workspace-analyst: that store reached 11.33 GB, of which 11 GB was
# lfs/objects (473 objects, largest ~0.59 GB). Verified as exact duplicates — an
# LFS object's filename IS the sha256 of its contents, and both of the two
# largest videos in work/hype_video/ hashed straight onto existing objects.
# Across all 19 dinomem workspaces: ~15.6 GB of duplicated media.
#
# WHY IGNORING IS THE RIGHT ANSWER, not just the cheaper one: a changelog exists
# to answer "what text changed, and let me undo it". A binary has no readable
# diff and no partial undo — you either keep the bytes or you do not, and keeping
# them is a BACKUP's job (procedures/backup.py, which now excludes this store).
# Owner, 2026-10-08: "it shld be auto changelog only ... no backup, no media".
#
# Ignoring also makes the size guard ABSOLUTE: with no LFS filter configured,
# nothing can be exempted from it again, which is the loophole that caused this.
#
# Need one of these versioned? Put it in a real project repo, or allowlist it
# via `.dinomem-keep-large` — do NOT re-add LFS rules to the snapshot store.

# Archives
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.zip
*.7z
*.rar
*.gz
*.bz2
*.xz
*.zst

# Images
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.webp
*.bmp
*.tiff
*.ico
*.psd
*.svgz

# Video / audio — the actual 11 GB in practice
*.mp4
*.mov
*.webm
*.mkv
*.avi
*.mp3
*.wav
*.ogg
*.flac
*.m4a

# Docs / fonts
*.pdf
*.ttf
*.otf
*.woff
*.woff2
*.eot

# Compiled / packaged blobs
*.wasm
*.so
*.dylib
*.dll
*.node
*.whl
*.deb
*.exe
*.bin
*.pack
*.onnx
*.safetensors
*.gguf
*.pt
*.pth
*.h5
*.npy
*.npz
*.parquet
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Media work dirs: whole-directory ignores, because a render pipeline emits
# hundreds of intermediate clips (183 .mp4 files in work/hype_video/ alone) and
# per-extension rules still leave the churn of frames/manifests behind.
work/*/clips/
work/*/clips_*/
work/*/archive_*/
workspace-*/work/*/clips/
workspace-*/work/*/clips_*/
workspace-*/work/*/archive_*/
# <<< dinomem git-autosnapshot ignores <<<
